  <!-- 5.2 -->
  <article class="example purple">
    <div class="ex-head"><span class="ex-tag">Example 5.2</span><h3>Intersection of Two Arrays II</h3><span class="lc">LeetCode 350</span></div>
    <p class="ex-problem">Given two integer arrays, return their intersection as a multiset — each element appears as many times as it appears in both inputs.</p>

    <div class="subhead bf"><span class="dot"></span> Brute Force · O(N·M) time · O(M) space</div>
    <p class="bf-note">For each element of <code>nums1</code>, linear-scan <code>nums2</code>. <b>Quadratic in the worst case.</b></p>

<pre class="code bf-code">def intersect_brute_force(nums1: list[int], nums2: list[int]) -&gt; list[int]:
    used = [False] * len(nums2)
    res = []
    for x in nums1:
        for j, y in enumerate(nums2):
            if not used[j] and x == y:
                used[j] = True
                res.append(x)
                break
    return res</pre>

    <div class="subhead opt"><span class="dot"></span> Optimal — Sort + Two-Array Merge · O(M log M + N log N) time · O(1) extra space*</div>

<pre class="code purple-code">def intersect(nums1: list[int], nums2: list[int]) -&gt; list[int]:
    <span class="cm"># Pre-requisite: sort both inputs</span>
    nums1.sort()
    nums2.sort()

    <span class="cm"># Primitive 1: One pointer per input, both at the start</span>
    i = 0
    j = 0
    res = []

    <span class="cm"># Primitive 2: Loop until either input is exhausted</span>
    while i &lt; len(nums1) and j &lt; len(nums2):
        <span class="cm"># Primitive 5: Compare the current pair</span>
        if nums1[i] == nums2[j]:
            res.append(nums1[i])
            <span class="cm"># Primitive 2: Match — advance BOTH pointers</span>
            i += 1
            j += 1
        elif nums1[i] &lt; nums2[j]:
            <span class="cm"># Primitive 2: nums1 is behind — catch it up</span>
            i += 1
        else:
            <span class="cm"># Primitive 2: nums2 is behind — catch it up</span>
            j += 1

    return res</pre>
    <p class="bf-note" style="margin-top:8px">* Sorting happens in place on the inputs (this mutates the caller's arrays) and Python's sort may use temporary memory; the extra space counted here excludes the sort and the output list.</p>

    <div class="subhead fc-purple"><span class="dot"></span> Algorithm Flowchart</div>
    <div class="flowchart-wrap">
      <div class="flowchart-title">Intersection of Two Arrays — Advance the Smaller</div>
      <svg class="flowchart-svg" viewBox="0 0 660 448" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Intersection of Two Arrays — Advance the Smaller">
        <rect x="224.9" y="10" width="210.2" height="34" rx="9" fill="#7c3aed"/>
        <text x="330" y="32" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">sort both; i = 0, j = 0</text>
        <path d="M330 44 L330 72" fill="none" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
        <polygon points="330,72 450,106 330,140 210,106" fill="#fff7ed" stroke="#f59e0b" stroke-width="1.6"/>
        <text x="330" y="102" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="800" fill="#b45309">i &lt; len1</text>
        <text x="330" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="800" fill="#b45309">AND j &lt; len2 ?</text>
        <path d="M330 140 L330 168" fill="none" stroke="#94a3b8" stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#ar)"/>
